  ],
  "question": {
    "text": "The number of relations, on the set&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced open=\"{\" close=\"}\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;containing&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;which are reflexive and transitive but not symmetric, is _________.",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "4",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Given,</p><p>Set&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"}\" open=\"{\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>Now Cartesian product&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"}\" open=\"{\"><mrow><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>Now, given the relation is reflexive,</p><p>So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8712;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>R</mi></math></p><p>Also given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#8712;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;must&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8712;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>R</mi></math></p><p>Now finding,&nbsp;<strong>Possible cases</strong> :</p><p><strong>Case-1</strong>: All of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8713;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;relation.</p><p><strong>Case-2: </strong>Only one of<strong>&nbsp;</strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#8712;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>3</mn></math>&nbsp;relations.</p><p>For example if relation is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced open=\"{\" close=\"}\"><mrow><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;then it is reflexive as well as transitive as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#8594;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>is&#160;present&#160;in&#160;relation</mtext></math></p><p>Note that exactly two of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#8712;</mo><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;not possible because if two of these&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8712;</mo><mi>R</mi></math>,&nbsp;third must&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8712;</mo><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;&nbsp;to make relation transitive.</p><p>Total number of relations&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math></p>",
    "image": null
  },
